    "result_text": "The full build passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`), with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization with minimal structural impact by adding one small cohesive unit and a two-line change to an already-small class \u2014 leaving the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` and `HouseholdDuplicates` untouched.\n\n**Changes (2 files):**\n\n1. **New `AddressNormalizer`** (`rest/advice/AddressNormalizer.java`) \u2014 a small standalone class with a single `normalize` method: trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-token abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). A `null`/blank input normalizes to `\"\"`.\n\n2. **`OwnerFieldsValidator.validate`** \u2014 normalizes the DTO's `address` at the top of validation, *before* the required-field loop.\n\n**Why this is low-impact and correct:**\n- The validator already mutates the DTO (telephone/email) and runs during request binding, *before* the controller creates the owner \u2014 so normalizing there means the normalized address flows through `ownerMapper.toOwner(...)` into the stored/returned `Owner` (satisfies \"stored and returned normalized\" and \"applied whenever an owner is created\").\n- Because normalization runs before the required-field check, a whitespace-only address becomes `\"\"` and is rejected by the existing `rejectIfEmptyOrWhitespace` \u2014 the blank-after-normalization rule.\n- Since the owner's stored address is already the normalized form, `HouseholdDuplicates` (duplicate detection and the SHA-256 household id, both of which read `owner.getAddress()`) automatically compares the normalized form \u2014 no edit needed there. This is what makes `\"12 MAIN ST\"` and `\"12 main street\"` collide into the same household.\n\nThis kept `WMC_other` tiny for every changed function (the new class has no surrounding complexity; the validator's other method is trivial) and avoided growing any large class.",
